Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
thank you very much for being on the  program well we just heard from  matt hancock what's your reaction to his  interview  well i'm angry to be honest i've been  watching  like everybody else the case rates  rising for several weeks  and seen every day this week we've  raised with the government  the fact that this new strain of the  virus surely means that christmas plans  have to be  revised and yet every day that concern  has been  dismissed ridiculed by the prime  minister in the house commons on  wednesday  only to find yesterday that people have  made plans over the last week that now  lie in tatters um with very little time  to sort that out before christmas and  that the virus has been allowed to  spread  over and over again we've seen the same  pattern a prime minister who rejects the  evidence  who ridicules and mocks people who raise  concerns who dithers and delays and then  ends up having to change his mind at the  11th hour this is how a country  that has pioneering scientists and a  healthcare system that is the envy of  the world  ends up with the worst number of deaths  in europe  and the worst recession of any major  economy we just cannot continue like  this  because what matt hancock and you know  the government would argue is that  they couldn't have predicted the new  variant they only got the  evidence uh on friday of  transmissibility  um i understand you know the the  difficulties that many have and that  anger may feel towards the way this  policy has been  communicated building up people's hopes  taking them away again  but is the policy itself the right thing  to do something that labor supports  it's absolutely the right thing to do to  bring in tougher restrictions  around christmas and i think most of the  country could see for several weeks that  this is where  it was going we didn't know about the  new strain of the varys a few weeks ago  but we did know that case rates were  rising  at an alarming rate in some parts of the  country in particular in london in the  south east  and matt hancock came to the house of  commons on monday to report that there  was this new strain of the virus which  he said at the time was particularly  virulent  the government did know that this was an  emerging problem and yet two days later  the prime minister  went to the house of commons mocked and  ridiculed concerns that were raised  about it  and said that those who were concerned  about it like kirsten the labour leader  were seeking to criminalize people and  to cancel christmas the trouble with all  of this is that it's the government that  is always  too slow to act too slow to act on the  evidence that is  in front of them in plain sight and  we've seen this over and over again  over the last 12 months and that is why  we're in  this situation right now going into the  new year  i didn't hear anything from matt hancock  that suggested that the government is  really going to get a grip on this we'll  have schools returning  in the first week or so of the new year  they've really got to start getting mass  testing rolled out in schools get a grip  on test trace and isolate  and make sure that they execute the  vaccination plan  properly so that we can get that rolled  out across the country  on schools specifically should the  government be be considering closing  schools  in tier 4 areas where the virus is  increasing  i think schools should be the last thing  to close and the first thing to open  we've said that all year and the reason  for that is because  we've seen what is happening to children  when they have prolonged periods of  absence  from school we'll be living with the  consequences of this for generations to  come  if we don't i think that's right so i  understand you think it should be  the last thing the last resort but is  that something that should be considered  as a last resort if infections are still  rising  well i think we should start focusing on  the first resort the first resort is  about getting mass testing  into schools and so far the government  has for a month has been  uh mucking about not getting that those  plans in place  we've had endless questions and debates  about that in the house of commons  and only on friday we had teachers uh  sent instructions about what they were  supposed to do over the school  holidays so after the one of the most  stressful years that i think any of them  can remember they're now working flat  out over the school holidays to try and  put new plans  in place for january we just can't  continue like this the government has  got to get mass testing into schools  they should try and keep schools open it  just cannot countenance the idea  that for some children they'll have what  is effectively almost a year  of missed education they will live with  those consequences for a long time to  come and the most disadvantaged  children will be the ones who suffer the  most we  just shouldn't be debating whether we  should be closing schools we should be  debating how we safely keep them open  the announcement yesterday of course  affected well everybody  in the country and millions of people  having their christmas plans  ruined many of us uh living under these  tier four restrictions for  well after hearing from matt hancock  potentially some months to come  do you think parliament needs to be  recalled so mps can vote on it  well there will be a vote as as matt  hancock said to you earlier there'll be  a vote at some point before  the 1st of february and i think the most  important thing right now  is just that there is clarity about the  new rules  and that we make sure that people  understand those and can follow them  safely  i don't think it'd be wise for mps to be  travelling across the country  into london in the south east which is  now subject to tier four  restrictions in order to debate  something that the government has the  power to do that they've said very  clearly  must be done in the last 24 hours we  finally got clarity we now need  to focus on making sure that people have  the support that they need to follow the  rules and to  to get to grips with what has been a  really chaotic  um set of announcements over the last 24  hours now that we've got clarity i think  that should absolutely be the priority  um i'm interested as well in how  strictly you think the rules  should be enforced um should the police  be able to hand out fines to people who  break the rules over christmas  if you knew your neighbor was breaking  the rules would you call the police  what's the position well here in the  north of england we've been  living under quite serious restrictions  since the summer and the police here  have largely  i think got it right in very difficult  circumstances they've taken a fairly  light  touch approach they haven't wanted to  bust in on  children's birthday parties and family  dinners but they have been very visible  they have been reminding people  about how serious this is when they need  to take action they do  but in general they're trusting people  they're asking people they're appealing  to people  to look out for one another and keep  each other safe and i think most of the  country is absolutely  trying to do that where we run into  problems is not so much  about enforcement or lack of enforcement  the problem is when people are confused  about what the rules are and that's why  it is so serious that the prime minister  on wednesday  was saying in the house of commons that  christmas would absolutely go ahead as  planned that there was  no chance at all it would be inhumane he  said to cancel  christmas and then just a few a few days  later  he's saying something different we just  cannot have a prime minister continuing  to make promises that he knows  he cannot keep we've seen this all year  we just need a  far better communication from this  government far more honesty  and matt hancock told me that he had  called his mum to say that you know  christmas was off  um how are you planning on spending  christmas  yeah i i called my mum as well actually  and keir had a  phone call with the prime minister  yesterday uh ahead of the announcement  and one of the first things i did was to  call my mum  uh just to to talk about christmas we  were always planning a very small  quiet family christmas we're in a  support bubble with my mum  and so we're planning to continue that  but it is  hard because i do have family members  who are going to be spending christmas  alone this year that's not something any  of us really wanted to see but we're  just trying to do what i think everybody  else is trying to do which is to say  you know this is just this has been one  hell of a year  and we've just got to chalk it up to  2020 this is what it's been like  and hopefully this time next year we'll  be in a far far better position if we  all stay safe and follow the rules and  look after each other  it does feel like quite a universal  reaction first thing you do is you want  to get on the phone to your mum to try  and work out what on earth  her to do in the situation that we're  all in  of course you know top of everyone's  mind this morning is the coverage  restrictions what it means for them and  their families but  it's right to touch on brexit as well  because we just got 11 days to the end  of the transition  if there is a deal is labour minded to  back it  we'd be minded to back it but we don't  know what it what would be in it  and we've had over the last few months  we've had a prime minister that has  broken promises over and over again that  has tried to use the  good friday agreement as a bargaining  chip in negotiations  and has threatened to tear up  international law  um in order to get his deal through so  we want to see that deal  first we don't trust that the prime  minister will come back with a deal  that's  in the national interest but we've  always said that we think that a deal  is absolutely essential no deal would be  a disaster for many businesses and  people across this country so  we would be minded to support it but we  need to see what's in it what we hope  most of all is that the government will  get its act together in these last 11  days and make sure that there is a deal  and that people aren't waking up on the  1st of january in the middle of a global  pandemic  with all of the chaos that a no deal  brexit would bring  it's interesting isn't it you know you  say that labour's always been of the  view that you'd like to see a deal  at the same time though you know if i  kind of rewind to the not too distant  past  you know you've always been someone  who's talked about the importance of  honoring  the result of the referendum you  seriously considered backing theresa  may's deal  at a time when keith starman was  actually arguing for a second referendum  is he finally catching up with you  i think it's more a question of of the  options that we've got in front of us  and confronting those  realities the truth is that the question  was fundamentally settled  last december at the general election  when the prime minister won  a majority an 80-seat majority with the  promise to get brexit done  the the option of a second referendum is  gone we we have left the european union  we left in january  so the option is do we have a deal or do  we not have a deal do we continue to  trade do our businesses continue to  thrive  can we continue to do well as a country  or are we going to allow the government  to crash us out of the eu without a deal  and hold the country back  and that you know on that i think the  labour party is completely united we  have got to  go forwards we've got to stop the  disunity and the division that has held  the country back over recent years and  we've got to get a good deal  and kira and i are absolutely of one  mind about that okay  lisa nundy thank you very much indeed  for being on the program thank you

